ña on November 19, 1809."}, {"sourceSlug": "battle-of-ocana", "sourceName": "Battle of Ocaña", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "french-occupation-of-andalusia", "targetName": "French Occupation of Andalusia", "context": "The Spanish defeat at the Battle of Ocaña directly facilitated the widespread French occupation of Andalusia in early 1810."}], "places": [{"name": "Hondarribia, Spain", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Ocaña, Spain", "role": "Site of major defeat"}, {"name": "Andalusia, Spain", "role": "Region impacted by defeat"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Juan Carlos de Aréizaga", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "juan-carlos-de-aréizaga"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3187624", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Carlos_Ar%C3%A9izaga"], "tags": ["Spain", "Military Leaders & Commanders", "Europe", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "empire-and-colonialism"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Juan Carlos de Aréizaga left a notable mark on Early Modern history, documented across 0 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries; Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Juan Carlos de Aréizaga's command and subsequent defeat at Ocaña significantly altered the strategic balance in the Peninsular War, leading to the rapid French occupation of southern Spain and profoundly impacting Spanish military and political leadership for years.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-27T11:19:35Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45988038092", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Juan Carlos Aréizaga y Alduncín (1756–1820) was a Spanish military commander.", "newValue": "Juan Carlos Aréizaga y Alduncín (1756–1820) was a Spanish military commander whose career became inextricably linked with the calamitous early years of the Peninsular War (1808–1814). Born in Hondarribia, Gipuzkoa, Aréizaga rose through the ranks of the Bourbon Spanish army, but his most significant, and ultimately tragic, command came during Spain's desperate struggle against Napoleonic France. His actions, particularly his leadership during the disastrous Battle of Ocaña in 1809, profoundly impacted the strategic landscape of the Iberian Peninsula, contributing to the prolonged French occupation and the subsequent restructuring of Spanish military command.

Elevated to command the Army of La Mancha in late 1809, Aréizaga was tasked with halting the advance of French forces under Marshal Soult and King Joseph Bonaparte. Despite commanding a numerically superior force of approximately 50,000 men, Aréizaga's inexperience in large-scale command and his perceived tactical indecision led to a catastrophic defeat at Ocaña on November 19, 1809. The Spanish army suffered over 18,000 casualties and the loss of nearly 50 cannons, effectively annihilating one of Spain's largest remaining field armies and opening the path for French control over much of Andalusia.

The devastating loss at Ocaña had profound consequences, significantly weakening Spain's ability to resist the French invasion independently and forcing a greater reliance on British forces under Wellington. Aréizaga's defeat contributed to a period of deep national demoralization and underscored the urgent need for military reform within the Supreme Central and Regency Councils. His subsequent court-martial, though inconclusive, marked the end of his active command and served as a stark lesson in the perils of appointing commanders based on political expediency rather than proven strategic acumen."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-27T11:19:35Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45988038092", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Early Modern era: age of exploration, printing revolution, and religious reformation", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1756-01-17, Hondarribia"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1756-01-17, Hondarribia"}], "newValue": ["The Napoleonic Wars created a widespread European conflict that engulfed Spain in the Peninsular War.", "The French invasion of Spain in 1808 prompted the formation of various Spanish armies, including the one Aréizaga would eventually command.", "The political instability and lack of experienced high command within the Spanish Supreme Central and Regency Councils led to Aréizaga's appointment to a major field army."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-27T11:19:35Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45988038092", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1820-03-18"}, {"title": "Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution", "type": "Movement", "year": "1820-03-18"}], "newValue": ["The catastrophic defeat at the Battle of Ocaña severely weakened Spain's military capacity and morale during the Peninsular War.", "The los